    :decision-status :open,
    :source-adjudication-references
    [{:id :package-resolution,
      :decision :qualified-alias,
      :target "behavior/pkg5/deterministic-dependency-solver",
      :rationale
      "PKG4 names resolve, requires deterministic resolution for the same inputs and explicitly delegates resolution to PKG5. PKG5 owns the same selection predicate with canonical inputs, specified tie-breakers, variants/features and complete direct/transitive identities. Retire only the redundant deterministic-resolution unit on central adoption; retain PKG4 operation entry and structured outputs under package-operation-artifacts.",
      :negative-distinctions
      ["A callable resolve command and operation records are not proved by a solver library result."
       "Choosing a dependency grants no authority; grant admission remains distinct."
       "Keep the resolve-name facet as operation-entry context as well as the qualified solver context."]}
     {:id :package-capability-diff,
      :decision :qualified-alias,
      :target "behavior/pkg4/package-update-diff",
      :rationale
      "PKG6's dependency effect/capability expansion report is the capability/effect projection of PKG4's pre-mutation update diff. Use one update transaction requirement with the full direct/transitive and profile/target context. Its acceptance must also reject expansion without policy review. Source/summary agreement remains owned by derived-capability-manifest and is retained as a shared facet, not absorbed as display-only behavior.",
      :negative-distinctions
      ["Displaying a diff does not grant or approve the expansion. Reject the update when required policy review is absent."
       "An unchanged direct dependency can contain a transitive effect/capability expansion."
       "Keep PKG6/4325-4389 source-analysis consistency with derived-capability-manifest; do not turn it into mere text output."]}
     {:id :registry-history,
      :decision :retain-distinct-qualified-refinement,
      :target "behavior/pkg4/yank-history",
      :rationale
      "PKG9 produces and retains visible registry yank/revocation and package-state records under public/private/latent access policy. PKG4 preserves content/identity metadata needed to rebuild an already locked package after a yank. They share historical-rebuild context but operate on different artifacts and consumers. Neither record retention nor reproducibility grants access or permits a revoked dependency under current policy.",
      :negative-distinctions
      ["A retained yank log alone does not preserve all material needed for an old lockfile."
       "Cached rebuild material alone does not publish the required registry revocation/state record."
       "Historical visibility must not leak private package existence; retained history is not ordinary resolution or revocation bypass."]}
     {:id :download-versus-use,
      :decision :retain-distinct-qualified-refinement,
      :target "behavior/pkg12/verify-before-consumption",
      :rationale
      "PKG4 verifies acquired package bytes against content identity and required registry signatures and rejects unverified downloads at acquisition/install. PKG12 is the actual consumer-use gate for the required content/schema/signature/SBOM/attestation bundle, including freshness, binding and revocation. Download verification is a qualified input to that gate, not an alias for every later use.",
      :negative-distinctions
      ["A correctly hashed/signed download can later have missing or stale mandatory attestation and must fail consumer use."
       "A consumer gate cannot retroactively make an unverified download/install conforming."
       "Keep PKG4's publish-policy facet as its qualified publication context; do not claim download hashing implements release admission."]}
     {:id :stage-admission,
      :decision :retain-distinct-with-concrete-existing-task-interface,
      :target
      "release/remaining-tasks-and-evidence/complete-stage-compatibilityequivalence-across-the-claimed-compiler",
      :rationale
      "BOOT5 produces the versioned support matrix and eligibility facts; TEST13 produces/runs the stage conformance, comparison and trust evidence and also requires rejection of unsupported advancement. The authoritative-admission unit is the single consuming decision for advancing a declared stage or stable authoritative module revision. TEST13's missing-pass advancement refusal is one predicate of this same admission consumer, not another gate or an alias of all conformance execution. It is not a second producer and is not identical to final release acceptance. Strengthen existing release task2 to execute the intermediate admission decision; keep task5 as the final release consumer.",
      :negative-distinctions
      ["A truthful matrix/manifest can describe an unsupported or incomplete stage without authorizing advancement."
       "Required applicable conformance failure, stale provenance/matrix or unexplained divergence denies authoritative admission."
       "Intermediate declared gaps may be owned or explicitly deferred within the claimed subset; only release candidates must have no unreviewed claimed-support gaps."
       "Ordinary source edits and focused tests are development, not authoritative migration and do not require a renewed admission gate."]}],
